The desk called this one known parser issue. That description does not cover every report.
This review uses the attached export, ending 4 October 2026. It does not claim that fixes shipped or that the live queue stopped.

## Separate work, counted once

<Investigation />

The first two groups have different failure paths. The third is a separately tracked investigation, not a proven third root cause.
The full ID lists above make the partition auditable. Each GitHub issue includes the same scoped count and evidence.

### Shared parser: day and month reversed

The exported `causeOf` rule says that the shared parser reads month first while these tenants use day-first dates.
For example, R-41200 says 7 April became 4 July, and R-41203 says 2 December became 12 February.
App and email reports belong here even for tenants running the newer booking form. Route by tenant configuration and entry channel, not the intake browser locale or subject line.

### Booking-v3: misleading field hint

The newer form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` but reads the tenant's day-first format. People follow the hint and the form interprets their entry differently.
This applies to form reports for Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour. R-41230 and R-41241 are examples.
Changing only the shared parser would leave this form path wrong. Align the hint, validation and parsing with the tenant format.

### One-day and temporal anomalies: evidence, not a diagnosis

`desk-notes.json` identifies R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344 and R-41620.
Read their bodies before assigning a cause:

- R-41202: Tuesday 12 May on the invite versus Monday 11 May on the calendar.
- R-41209: June bookings are one day off; no dates or reproduction steps were supplied.
- R-41236: an invite reportedly arrived before booking; this is a temporal anomaly, not proof of a one-day shift.
- R-41344: Lisbon and Toronto recipients saw different days for the same meeting.
- R-41620: Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday.

These observations do not establish a month/day reversal. Time-zone conversion, date-only storage and scheduling order are hypotheses to test, not confirmed causes.
Collect original inputs, stored instants, named time zones, offsets, expected local dates and delivery timestamps. Reproduce each path before choosing a fix.

## What the old component actually shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The exported component places the anomalous reports under parser based on tenant and channel. Its derived table is a classification result, not independent proof of the body text.
The partition at the top separates those named reports without silently rewriting the original rules or data.
</Callout>

Intake tags disagree with the tenant/form classification. A tag is a guess made at intake, not a cause.
Reports with the same subject can require different fixes, while different subjects can describe the same failure.

## The queue did not begin at the merge

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived from April. The large July merge is a desk action, not a July incident onset or proof of resolution.
Track received time separately from merged time and keep reading new reports while work is queued. This chart ends at the export boundary.

## Replace the subject-only rule

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) and [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) were reviewed in June 2024.
Their text says to merge by subject; their executable examples distinguish causes by tenant and channel.
The runbook marks these pages stale. Their accepted decision is preserved as history; the proposed successor records a new policy.

<DedupeExplorer />

See [decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-evidence). Triage should inspect the report body, tenant locale, form version and channel, then deduplicate by a reproduced failure path.
Keep anomalous reports separate until evidence supports a merge. Do not label an entire ticket fixed merely because its largest group has a queued fix.

## Acceptance and follow-up

- Parser: day-first inputs preserve the intended calendar date through app and email paths; month-first tenants keep their intended behavior.
- Hint: booking-v3 hint, validation and date interpretation agree for each tenant; test ambiguous and unambiguous dates.
- Anomalies: establish expected dates in both named time zones and show whether a reproducible fault or expected conversion explains each report.
- Desk: reconcile every unique report ID once, retain uncertainty and refresh counts when another export arrives.

